  <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-course-policies">
    <title>Course Policies</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Extra Credit</title>
      <p>
        There will be no extra credit available in this course. 
        If you are interested in raising your grade, take the time you would have spent on an extra credit assignment and spend it making sure you get more "regular credit" instead: 
        master a topic you've found difficult, find exercises in the textbook to work on, spend a few extra hours studying, etc.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Studying and Coursework</title>
      <p>
        To succeed in this course, you will need to dedicate a significant amount of time to working on calculus. 
        Mathematics is learned by doing, meaning it is important to work numerous problems to fully understand the concepts we cover. 
        The assigned homework is the minimum amount you should be completing; if you feel like you don't fully understand a concept, work extra problems from a textbook or ask me for some additional practice problems. 
        This is a 4-credit summer course, which means there should be 8 learning hours per week (a fully face-to-face section of the course would be required to meet 8 hours per week). 
        A general rule in all college courses is for each hour of class per week you spend a minimum of two hours outside of class on homework, studying, reading the text, etc. 
        Since this course has 8 online learning hours per week, you should plan to spend an additional 16 hours (for a total of at least 24 hours) working on a calculus per week.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Accomodations</title>
      <p>
        If you anticipate issues related to the format or requirements of this course, please contact me as early as possible to discuss ways to ensure your full participation. 
        If you believe formal, disability-related accommodations are appropriate, it is very important you register with the Student Accessibility Services office 
        and notify me of your eligibility for such accommodations. 
        We can then plan how to best coordinate your accommodations.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
    <title>Academic Integrity and AI Usage</title>
      <p>
        The WSU Mathematics Department does not tolerate academic dishonesty! For an explanation of what is considered "academic dishonesty" at 
        WSU, please see the policy included in the <url href="https://catalog.worcester.edu/undergraduate/academic-policies-procedures/academic-honesty">2025-2026 
        Undergraduate Catalog</url>. All instructors in the WSU Mathematics Department are required to 
        report all incidents of academic dishonesty observed, detected, or otherwise determined to have occurred to the WSU Academic Central File. 
        WSU requires some penalty be imposed for every reported violation of the academic honesty policy. All incidents of academic dishonesty in 
        this course will result in (at least) a score of 0 on that assignment; additional penalties will be imposed at the instructor's discretion 
        and/or as required by the 
        <url href="https://webcdn.worcester.edu/mathematics/wp-content/uploads/sites/17/2023/03/Math-Department-Academic-Honesty-Policy.pdf">Mathematics 
        Department Academic Honesty Policy</url>. In this course, instances of academic dishonesty will include (but not limited to!):
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Submitting any assignment you did not complete yourself, either work produced by a classmate/friend or from an online resources 
            (such as an online calculator) or third party (such as Chegg, Course Hero, etc.).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Using course materials/notes, the internet, or a calculator/other device in any unauthorized way during an online quiz or an in-person exam.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Sharing the contents of a quiz with a classmate.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Generative artificial intelligence (AI), such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, etc., should not be used in any way to complete graded assignments in this course. 
        Any evidence of generative AI use on an assignment will be considered an academic honesty violation and will result in at least a 0 on that assignment.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Instructor Rights</title>
      <p>
        <alert> As the instructor of this course, I reserve the right to make changes to the above course policies at any time. All changes will be announced in class.</alert>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    
  </subsubsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-slos-lasc">
    <title>Student Learning Outcomes and LASC Information</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Student Learning Outcomes</title>
      <p>
        After successfully completing this course, students will be able to...
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Apply different techniques of integration, including substitution, integration by parts, partial fractions, and trigonometric substitution.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Use the definite integral in a variety of applications, such as volumes of solids of revolution, arc length of curves, and separable differential equations.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Determine absolute and conditional convergence of series using a variety of methods including the integral test, comparison test, limit comparison test, alternating series test, ratio test, and root test.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Perform operations on power series to construct related power series.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Compute Taylor series of functions and their intervals of convergence.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Liberal Arts and Sciences Curriculum (LASC)</title>
      <p>
        This course counts toward the Quantitative Reasoning (QR) category of the Liberal Arts and Sciences Curriculum (LASC).
        The course addresses the following LASC Quantitative Reasoning objectives:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Acquaint students with formal systems, procedures, and sequences of operations.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Strengthen students' understanding of variables and functions.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Apply mathematical techniques to the analysis and solution of real-life problems.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Emphasize the importance of accuracy, including precise language and careful definitions of mathematical concepts.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Understand both the underlying principles and practical applications of one or more fields of mathematics.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Strengthen understanding of the relationship between algebraic and graphical representations.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        and the following LASC Overarching objectives:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Communicate effectively orally and in writing.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Understand and employ quantitative and qualitative reasoning.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Apply skills in critical thinking.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Understand the roles of science and technology in our modern world.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Make connections across courses and disciplines.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Develop as healthy individuals - physically, emotionally, socially, ethically, and intellectually.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>
  </subsection>

